-- title:
--   Finite points are the A-extendable m-torsion classes
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $A$ (so $A$ lies over $p$) whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$. Let $\Lambda$ be level data over $A$, providing in particular a structure morphism $\sigma_A : \operatorname{Spec} A \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ with $\mathrm{barPt}\,A$ followed by $\sigma_A$ equal to the generic point, and let $O$ be a Néron object at $p$ for this data: a scheme $G$ with structure morphism $g$, a relative group law $L$ on $g$, and a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ between $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field $\mathrm{xHFunctionFieldBar}\,M\,H$ and the sections of $g$ over the generic point, together with the further data and axioms of `JHNeronObjectAtP`. Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$. Four assertions are made. First, a class $x$ lies in `O.finPts m`, the subgroup generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose associated generic section extends over $\sigma_A$, if and only if $x$ itself is killed by $m$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and $O.\mathrm{pts}\,x$ factors as $\mathrm{barPt}\,A$ followed by some section of $g$ over $\sigma_A$; that is, the generating set is already a subgroup. Second, if a section $s$ of $g$ over $\sigma_A$ satisfies $m \cdot s = e$ for $L$, then the class corresponding under $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to the generic restriction of $s$ lies in `O.finPts m`. Third, if $x \in$ `O.finPts m` and $s$ is a section over $\sigma_A$ with $(O.\mathrm{pts}\,x)$ equal to $\mathrm{barPt}\,A$ followed by $s$, then $m \cdot s = e$. Fourth, if $m \cdot s = e$ and $s_\kappa$ is a section over $\mathrm{resPt}\,A$ followed by $\sigma_A$ obtained by composing with $\mathrm{resPt}\,A$, then $m \cdot s_\kappa = e$.
--
--   This identifies the finite part of the $m$-torsion of the Néron object at a prime $p$ dividing the level with the $m$-torsion classes whose points extend over the valuation ring $A$, and transfers the $m$-torsion condition between a class, its $A$-section and the induced point over the residue field. It is the level-$\Gamma_H(M)$ counterpart of the corresponding statement for $\Gamma_0(N_0p)$, and is used in the construction of the toric filtration on the Tate module of the Néron object and in the comparison of generic and special points.
